description:
Write `CTL_CODE` and its inverse from lesson 2.03, and the access check the I/O manager makes before an IOCTL reaches the driver:

```cpp
struct Ioctl { std::uint32_t deviceType, access, function, method; };

std::uint32_t ctlCode(std::uint32_t deviceType, std::uint32_t function, std::uint32_t method, std::uint32_t access);
Ioctl decode(std::uint32_t code);
std::string describe(std::uint32_t code);
bool accessAllowed(std::uint32_t code, std::uint32_t handleAccess);   // handleAccess: bit 0 read, bit 1 write
```

- `ctlCode` is `(deviceType << 16) | (access << 14) | (function << 2) | method`.
- `describe` gives `"device 0x8000, function 0x800, METHOD_BUFFERED, FILE_ANY_ACCESS"`: hex in upper case with at least 4 digits for the device and 3 for the function, method names `METHOD_BUFFERED`, `METHOD_IN_DIRECT`, `METHOD_OUT_DIRECT`, `METHOD_NEITHER`, access names `FILE_ANY_ACCESS` (0), `FILE_READ_DATA` (1), `FILE_WRITE_DATA` (2), `FILE_READ_DATA|FILE_WRITE_DATA` (3).
- `accessAllowed`: every access bit the code requires must be in `handleAccess`.

